WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.415 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.415 --> 00:00:04.347 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.347 --> 00:00:06.279 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:10.933 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:12.100 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: All right.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, welcome to the
first math lecture.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.400 align:middle line:84%
The topic today
is linear algebra.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.320 align:middle line:84%
And what I expect is
that, for most of you,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.660 align:middle line:84%
the topics will
be very familiar.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:31.680 align:middle line:84%
At least we'll be covering basic
fundamentals of vector algebra,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.860 align:middle line:84%
then dealing with
special matrices,

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.280 align:middle line:84%
matrices that are used in
applications, eigenvectors

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:46.020 align:middle line:84%
and eigenvalue, decompositions,
singular value decompositions,

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.080 align:middle line:84%
and then, finally, the
Perron-Frobenius theorem.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:56.080 align:middle line:84%
And what's really neat
about quantitative finance

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.380 align:middle line:84%
is that linear
algebra can really

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.100 align:middle line:84%
be very useful in understanding
different computations we

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.300 align:middle line:90%
want to do.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.300 align:middle line:90%
So it makes our life easier.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's just begin
with a discussion

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.220 align:middle line:84%
of basic linear
algebra concepts.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.180 align:middle line:84%
I'll use a notation
where often I'll

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.140 align:middle line:90%
have a bold vector notation--

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.660 align:middle line:84%
or bold letter for
noting a vector.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:31.820 align:middle line:84%
So this bold v is a vector
in m-dimensional space.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.220 align:middle line:84%
And it just consists of an
ordered list of numbers.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:41.700 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of it as
a column vector typically.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.260 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, we'll think
of it as a row vector.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.180 align:middle line:84%
But special cases of vectors
will be the vector of all

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.980 align:middle line:90%
0's and the vector of all 1's.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:58.600 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of a
graphical representation

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:05.000 align:middle line:84%
of a vector, where
we can think of it

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.400 align:middle line:84%
as perhaps just a single
point in, in this case,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.680 align:middle line:90%
two-dimensional space.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:20.960 align:middle line:84%
Or with an origin here, we might
think of the vector representing

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:26.760 align:middle line:84%
the directed line segment
from the origin to that point,

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.400 align:middle line:90%
where this is v1 and this is v2.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.730 align:middle line:84%
Depending on our application,
both of these can be useful.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.400 align:middle line:90%


00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:44.800 align:middle line:84%
And a basic example for us
could be the closing prices

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.080 align:middle line:84%
on a given day for all
stocks in the S&P 500 index.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:57.300 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of p boldface
being a vector of 500 values

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.340 align:middle line:84%
corresponding to the
closing price of stocks

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.380 align:middle line:90%
that are in the S&P 500 index.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.420 align:middle line:84%
Importantly, the
prices are positive.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.140 align:middle line:84%
So that's where the R
plus comes into play.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:21.340 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider a portfolio
holding shares of the S&P 500

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:27.780 align:middle line:84%
stocks, we can represent
that with a vector q.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.980 align:middle line:84%
And the value of such a
portfolio at the end of the day

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.660 align:middle line:84%
is simply the sum of the
quantities, the shares

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.110 align:middle line:90%
times the price per share.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:46.780 align:middle line:90%


00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:55.360 align:middle line:84%
And later on, we'll see
that q dot p, the dot

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.040 align:middle line:84%
product of those two
vectors, gives us

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.946 align:middle line:90%
the value of the portfolio.

00:04:00.946 --> 00:04:03.480 align:middle line:90%


00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.880 align:middle line:84%
What we're going to find
useful is to actually

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:15.960 align:middle line:84%
add to our 500 stocks the
value of a cash holding

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.620 align:middle line:90%
in the portfolio.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:26.480 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of p0 of
t just being a fixed $1.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.480 align:middle line:84%
And then q0 tells us how
many dollars we hold.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:42.640 align:middle line:84%
And so our portfolio value, vt,
will be given by this number.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.720 align:middle line:84%
And we can think about
rebalancing portfolios,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.580 align:middle line:84%
computing portfolio and
loss on a daily basis.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:05:00.060 align:middle line:84%
And we can characterize
rebalancing the portfolio

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:07.180 align:middle line:84%
as shifting from shares
held at the end of day t

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:16.140 align:middle line:84%
to qj of t plus 1 to be
held through day t plus 1

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:22.660 align:middle line:84%
by adjusting the quantity
for each stock by a delta j.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:30.740 align:middle line:84%
And if we constrain the
sum of the change in shares

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:36.740 align:middle line:84%
across the j stocks
and cash and the pj,

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:41.140 align:middle line:84%
multiplying by the pj of t,
we can constrain this sum

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.220 align:middle line:90%
to be equal to 0.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:49.840 align:middle line:84%
So this would correspond to a
rebalancing of the portfolio

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:57.440 align:middle line:84%
and basically not taking any of
distributions from the account.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:07.360 align:middle line:84%
Now, with the net gain, let's
see, this terminology, PnL,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.960 align:middle line:84%
it's, in a sense, a
misnomer, I think,

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.160 align:middle line:84%
because it's really
profit and loss.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.040 align:middle line:84%
But it's referred to as
PnL, portfolio net gain.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:22.440 align:middle line:84%
We can just look at the
change in value from day t

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.480 align:middle line:90%
to day t plus 1.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:31.360 align:middle line:84%
And this will have this simple
form for the change in portfolio

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:31.940 align:middle line:90%
value--

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.280 align:middle line:84%
basically, the shares that
are held from the end of day

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.160 align:middle line:84%
t to t plus 1 times the
change in price from day t

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.200 align:middle line:90%
to day t plus 1.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:48.020 align:middle line:84%
And so this is the
portfolio net gain.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:53.900 align:middle line:84%
And here, we're talking
about portfolio management

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.660 align:middle line:84%
maybe on a daily basis,
where, at the end of the day,

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:02.660 align:middle line:84%
we make a decision as how
to rebalance our portfolio.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:08.220 align:middle line:84%
And there's a net gain
during that period.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:13.300 align:middle line:84%
One could adjust the
period of rebalancing

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:18.020 align:middle line:84%
to be intraday on a
regular or irregular basis.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.500 align:middle line:84%
And we'd have to have
extra notation to do that.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:36.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, with vector concepts, if we
multiply vector v by a scalar c,

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:40.380 align:middle line:84%
then we just multiply through
each of the components of v

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:41.980 align:middle line:90%
by that scalar.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:46.680 align:middle line:84%
And if we add two
vectors, we simply

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:51.960 align:middle line:84%
comprise the sum of
components element by element.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.640 align:middle line:84%
And the dot product
between two vectors

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:59.920 align:middle line:84%
is simply the sum of
the product of the two.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:07.520 align:middle line:84%
So this allows us to work
with vector algebra associated

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.320 align:middle line:90%
with portfolios.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:16.920 align:middle line:84%
If we have two portfolios,
we can consider, let's see--

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:28.280 align:middle line:84%
we can think of a qt and,
in this notation, a wt

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:31.480 align:middle line:90%
as two different portfolios.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:35.240 align:middle line:84%
And with each of
these portfolios,

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:43.549 align:middle line:84%
we can determine what the profit
and loss is of each portfolio.

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:47.670 align:middle line:84%
So for q, it's the
value at the end of day

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:51.230 align:middle line:84%
minus the value at the beginning
of the day and similarly

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:53.590 align:middle line:90%
for portfolio w.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:09:00.750 align:middle line:84%
And it's useful to include
in the notation here

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:07.710 align:middle line:84%
this BOD and EOD to represent
begining of day, end of day.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:18.870 align:middle line:84%
And what's relevant
actually is that the qt

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:26.710 align:middle line:84%
portfolio composition,
while it's indexed by t,

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:32.590 align:middle line:84%
it's actually determined at
the end of day t minus 1.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:39.150 align:middle line:84%
And so one needs to be
careful in doing computations

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:44.690 align:middle line:84%
with portfolio value
measurement that you're always

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:49.210 align:middle line:84%
using portfolios that are
defined using only information

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.250 align:middle line:90%
up to a given time point.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.170 align:middle line:84%
So if we're going
to be rebalancing

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:58.730 align:middle line:84%
at the end of the day for
investment the next day,

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:03.850 align:middle line:84%
we can't know what will
happen on the next day.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:04.390 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:11.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, with these portfolios
that are just simple vectors,

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.890 align:middle line:90%
we can look at their difference.

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:22.550 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look at dt
equal to qt minus wt,

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.370 align:middle line:90%
this defines another portfolio.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:30.850 align:middle line:84%
We often constrain
portfolios to be long only,

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:37.450 align:middle line:84%
meaning the q's and
w's are positive or 0.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:44.150 align:middle line:84%
But there's no reason why we
can't allow component weights

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:47.090 align:middle line:90%
in portfolios to be negative.

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:49.710 align:middle line:84%
And in this case,
it's negative shares.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:11:00.390 align:middle line:84%
And so one can have a long short
portfolio that is given by d.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:05.750 align:middle line:84%
And we can calculate the profit
and loss of that d portfolio

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:11.990 align:middle line:84%
by subtracting the profit
and loss of the q and w.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:18.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's see, with short
selling in markets, let's see--

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:22.150 align:middle line:84%
who in the class might
offer a description of what

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:25.610 align:middle line:90%
it means to sell short a stock?

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:36.230 align:middle line:84%
So suppose that we have dt sub 2
is equal to, say, negative 200.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:40.650 align:middle line:90%
What would that mean?

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:44.450 align:middle line:84%
Or can we actually
realize a portfolio

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:50.180 align:middle line:84%
where we're short one of
the stocks by 200 shares?

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.810 align:middle line:90%


00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:54.830 align:middle line:90%
I'm sure someone--

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:55.570 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:56.430 align:middle line:90%
What's your name?

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:56.930 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Alexander.

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:58.170 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: OK,
thank you, Alexander.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.670 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I believe it is when
you sell someone else's stock,

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:02.773 align:middle line:84%
and then you buy it
back [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:02.773 --> 00:12:03.690 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes.

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:07.190 align:middle line:84%
You basically sell
first, buy back later.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:08.690 align:middle line:90%
And you sell by--

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:10.270 align:middle line:90%
you don't have the stock.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:15.010 align:middle line:84%
So you borrow it
from some seller.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:20.370 align:middle line:84%
And brokerage accounts
that have margin

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:26.010 align:middle line:84%
allow you to engage in
this kind of transaction.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:30.930 align:middle line:84%
And so the policy
of the brokerage

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:34.410 align:middle line:84%
is that if you want
to sell short a stock,

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:39.190 align:middle line:84%
they need to locate some
shares for you to borrow.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:44.790 align:middle line:84%
And what's rather neat
about that selling short

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:48.710 align:middle line:84%
is that your brokerage
account gets credited

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:52.030 align:middle line:90%
with the sale of those shares.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:57.670 align:middle line:84%
So your brokerage account
will have a positive cash

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.430 align:middle line:90%
flow due to the short selling.

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:06.590 align:middle line:84%
And with such a positive
contribution to the brokerage

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:11.070 align:middle line:84%
account, you can
actually invest long

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:14.270 align:middle line:84%
in more than, say,
100% of your wealth

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:18.590 align:middle line:84%
that's in the-- or of your bank
account or brokerage account.

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:26.670 align:middle line:84%
So that's quite advantageous
and allows for very interesting

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.030 align:middle line:90%
trading opportunities.

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:29.610 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:35.650 align:middle line:84%
Well, an important special
case of a long short portfolio

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:39.170 align:middle line:90%
is a zero-cost portfolio.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:45.130 align:middle line:84%
And this would be one where
the sum of basically the values

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:49.490 align:middle line:84%
of the long and short positions
balance each other perfectly.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:56.170 align:middle line:84%
And so we have a
portfolio, dt in this case,

00:13:56.170 --> 00:14:00.450 align:middle line:84%
where the d components
are not all 0.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:06.210 align:middle line:84%
But the value, the net value of
the portfolio is, in fact, 0.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:14.890 align:middle line:84%
And we can hope for the
possibility of an arbitrage

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:21.890 align:middle line:84%
portfolio, which could be a
portfolio that has zero cost,

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:30.550 align:middle line:84%
but has a positive profit
and loss over a period.

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:36.750 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of
opportunity, generally--

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:41.670 align:middle line:84%
well, it doesn't exist
in efficient markets.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:45.630 align:middle line:84%
But markets are never
completely efficient.

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:51.950 align:middle line:84%
And so it can be
feasible to identify

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:56.230 align:middle line:84%
portfolios that may be close
to an arbitrage portfolio.

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:59.230 align:middle line:90%


00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:05.870 align:middle line:84%
What, in fact, happens is
that this profit and loss

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:10.950 align:middle line:84%
of a zero-cost portfolio
will be random.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:15.430 align:middle line:84%
And depending upon
the market model

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:20.830 align:middle line:84%
that applies to the
market we're trading in,

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:23.390 align:middle line:84%
it may be that there
are opportunities

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:29.910 align:middle line:84%
that yield a positive profit
all the time, in which case

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:32.690 align:middle line:84%
we would say that's an
arbitrage opportunity.

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:37.010 align:middle line:84%
What we'll see later
today is the question of,

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:45.090 align:middle line:84%
under what conditions is there
no arbitrage in a given market?

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:51.970 align:middle line:84%
And how do we characterize
prices of assets in a market

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:54.010 align:middle line:90%
with no arbitrage?

00:15:54.010 --> 00:16:01.890 align:middle line:84%
And ultimately, it turns out
that option pricing theory, that

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:06.010 align:middle line:84%
was mentioned last time of
Black, Scholes, and Merton,

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:11.730 align:middle line:84%
is all based upon arbitrage-free
markets and the implications

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:13.050 align:middle line:90%
of that.

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:18.410 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see that, in
arbitrage-free markets,

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:22.470 align:middle line:84%
there does not exist any
arbitrage portfolios.

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:26.520 align:middle line:84%
And what will be neat is to
understand why that's the case.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.990 align:middle line:90%


00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:30.490 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:36.510 align:middle line:84%
So we have random prices
at the end of day t.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.960 align:middle line:84%
And so the profit and loss
is going to be random.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:42.350 align:middle line:90%


00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:46.790 align:middle line:84%
I guess it's relevant to note
that there are strategies

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:49.550 align:middle line:90%
of statistical arbitrage.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:57.310 align:middle line:84%
And those are strategies where
we have portfolio specifications

00:16:57.310 --> 00:17:02.390 align:middle line:84%
d-star for which this
random profit and loss is

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:06.430 align:middle line:84%
a distribution that tends to
generate positive returns.

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:11.430 align:middle line:84%
And so there's a statistical
likelihood of making money.

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:15.230 align:middle line:84%
But it's often not
a pure arbitrage,

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:18.829 align:middle line:84%
but what we might call
a statistical arbitrage.

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:19.410 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:25.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's review just some
other concepts of vectors.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:34.050 align:middle line:84%
We have the norm of a vector
being the length of a vector.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:44.890 align:middle line:84%
So if we have a vector v
here, and the norm of v

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:50.410 align:middle line:84%
is basically the
length of this vector.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:56.810 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider what the dot
product is between two vectors,

00:17:56.810 --> 00:18:03.390 align:middle line:84%
we have this cosine formula
for what the dot product is.

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:08.410 align:middle line:84%
It's actually the length
of v times the length

00:18:08.410 --> 00:18:13.970 align:middle line:84%
of w times the cosine of
the angle between the two.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:23.010 align:middle line:84%
So if we have another
vector w here, then

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:27.310 align:middle line:84%
the dot product corresponds
to that formula.

00:18:27.310 --> 00:18:32.870 align:middle line:84%
And what's interesting about
that formula is that it actually

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:35.910 align:middle line:90%
corresponds to--

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:42.310 align:middle line:84%
the dot product corresponds
to the length of the segment

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:48.190 align:middle line:90%
along w that is closest to v.

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:54.430 align:middle line:84%
And so with this dot
product notation,

00:18:54.430 --> 00:19:00.190 align:middle line:84%
we actually can talk about
orthogonality between vectors

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:05.670 align:middle line:90%
in m-dimensional space.

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:09.790 align:middle line:90%
And those can be very useful.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:14.190 align:middle line:84%
We'll actually see
this orthogonality

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.550 align:middle line:84%
applied when we talk
about regression

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:19.490 align:middle line:90%
and least squares regression.

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:21.690 align:middle line:84%
So you may be familiar
with some of these topics

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:24.610 align:middle line:90%
from that discussion.

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:25.610 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:31.890 align:middle line:84%
Well, with vectors, a
really important topic

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:38.210 align:middle line:84%
is whether vectors are linearly
independent of each other.

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:43.210 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have
two vectors, v and w,

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:49.570 align:middle line:84%
they are linearly independent
if the only linear combination

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:56.050 align:middle line:84%
of the two which gives us the 0
vector is the linear combination

00:19:56.050 --> 00:20:00.010 align:middle line:90%
with c1 equals c2 equals 0.

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:08.650 align:middle line:84%
So linear independence
is essentially the case

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:14.290 align:middle line:84%
that we have a v and
w that aren't aligned

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:19.430 align:middle line:84%
with each other
in two dimensions,

00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:23.830 align:middle line:84%
at least vectors emanating from
the origin having that property.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:26.790 align:middle line:84%
And the utility of
linear independence

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:36.950 align:middle line:84%
is that we can work with a
vector space, capital S here.

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:40.590 align:middle line:84%
And the definition
of a vector space

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:44.630 align:middle line:84%
is that any vector
can be rescaled

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:46.350 align:middle line:90%
by an arbitrary constant c.

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:48.350 align:middle line:90%
And it's still in the space.

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:51.290 align:middle line:84%
And with any two vectors,
v and w, in the space,

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:52.430 align:middle line:90%
their sum is in the space.

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:55.350 align:middle line:90%


00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:59.430 align:middle line:84%
With a vector space,
what's very useful is

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:06.950 align:middle line:84%
a basis for the space, which
corresponds to linearly

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:13.070 align:middle line:84%
independent vectors, v1
to vp, such that the only

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:16.510 align:middle line:84%
linear combination
giving the 0 vector

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:19.410 align:middle line:84%
is the linear combination
where all the coefficients

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:22.170 align:middle line:90%
are equal to 0.

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:22.870 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:25.450 align:middle line:90%


00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:32.330 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's just go through all
of matrix algebra, pretty much.

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:37.050 align:middle line:84%
We can extend from
vectors to matrices

00:21:37.050 --> 00:21:43.050 align:middle line:84%
where we have m rows and
n columns in a matrix A,

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:45.810 align:middle line:90%
a rectangular matrix.

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:52.090 align:middle line:84%
And one very useful
representation of a matrix

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:56.050 align:middle line:84%
is as a collection
of column vectors

00:21:56.050 --> 00:21:59.490 align:middle line:84%
that are stacked
one after the other.

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:05.930 align:middle line:84%
And so we can denote bold
a sub 1 through bold a sub

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:10.330 align:middle line:90%
n as the column vectors.

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:18.110 align:middle line:84%
And with matrix
algebra, let's see,

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:24.910 align:middle line:84%
well, we can multiply a matrix,
capital A, by a constant.

00:22:24.910 --> 00:22:30.470 align:middle line:84%
And we end up multiplying each
of the components, elements

00:22:30.470 --> 00:22:35.790 align:middle line:84%
of the table of A values
by the same constant c.

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:39.470 align:middle line:84%
With a transpose
operation, a transpose

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:43.590 align:middle line:84%
is simply to flip
rows and columns.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:50.590 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have a sub ij
being the i-th row j-th column

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:56.070 align:middle line:84%
element of A, then Aji
corresponds to the j-th row

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:58.790 align:middle line:90%
and i-th column.

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:03.030 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we think
of the transpose

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:09.470 align:middle line:84%
of each of the column vectors
in A, that's a row vector.

00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:14.730 align:middle line:84%
And our transpose
of the matrix A

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:18.370 align:middle line:84%
can be thought of as
stacked row vectors

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:23.450 align:middle line:84%
corresponding to the same
vectors, a1 through an.

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:27.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, one of the reasons
for thinking of a matrix

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:32.490 align:middle line:84%
as being a composition of
different column vectors

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:40.690 align:middle line:84%
is that, when we multiply
a matrix A by a vector v,

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:47.650 align:middle line:84%
then this corresponds to
the linear combination

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:55.330 align:middle line:84%
of the columns of A with
coefficients corresponding to v1

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:56.770 align:middle line:90%
through vn.

00:23:56.770 --> 00:24:01.290 align:middle line:84%
So we have a linear
combination of A's columns.

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:08.530 align:middle line:84%
And this representation of
matrix vector multiplication

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:12.790 align:middle line:84%
turns out to be
very, very useful.

00:24:12.790 --> 00:24:21.310 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps less useful is that,
if we look at the matrix vector

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:29.830 align:middle line:84%
product, it's also a vector of
dot products of A's rows with v.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:33.910 align:middle line:84%
And so being comfortable
with dot products

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:37.750 align:middle line:90%
is potentially useful.

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:41.750 align:middle line:84%
But for the moment, thinking
of linear combinations

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:47.070 align:middle line:90%
of columns of A is very useful.

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:55.150 align:middle line:84%
So I guess, if we think of
the columns of A corresponding

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:58.510 align:middle line:84%
to potentially a
basis of vectors,

00:24:58.510 --> 00:25:05.110 align:middle line:84%
then Av corresponds to another
vector in the space spanned

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:08.430 align:middle line:90%
by those columns.

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:08.930 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:14.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we multiply
two matrices together,

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:20.420 align:middle line:84%
we actually have a
matrix A and a matrix B,

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:24.700 align:middle line:84%
each with their respective
columns, vectors.

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:30.940 align:middle line:84%
And we can basically expand the
matrix B into b1 through bp,

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:34.310 align:middle line:84%
and then do the matrix
vector product for each term.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:37.340 align:middle line:90%


00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:42.260 align:middle line:84%
This is equivalent, actually,
to looking at element Cij, which

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:44.780 align:middle line:90%
are just these double sums.

00:25:44.780 --> 00:25:50.220 align:middle line:84%
When I learned linear
algebra decades ago,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:55.460 align:middle line:84%
I used these double sums,
which were not very intuitive

00:25:55.460 --> 00:26:02.220 align:middle line:84%
to interpret, although one
with a simple algebra notation

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:03.520 align:middle line:90%
can prove everything.

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:07.920 align:middle line:84%
But things are much easier to
prove with this other notation.

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:12.400 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's important when we
multiply two matrices together

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:16.720 align:middle line:84%
is that the matrices
have to be conformal,

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:22.560 align:middle line:84%
meaning we need to be able to
multiply A times the column

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:33.560 align:middle line:84%
vectors of B. So the
m by n matrix of A

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:45.360 align:middle line:84%
has to be able to multiply the
m by p matrix of B. Actually,

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:46.540 align:middle line:90%
I think there's a--

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:49.462 align:middle line:90%


00:26:49.462 --> 00:26:51.880 align:middle line:90%
all right, let's see.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:52.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, sorry.

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:58.280 align:middle line:84%
And there are n columns
in A and n rows in B.

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:03.200 align:middle line:84%
And so the result
is an m by p matrix.

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:05.540 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:10.280 align:middle line:84%
Well, with different
matrix operations,

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:13.540 align:middle line:84%
you learn in linear
algebra all of these rules,

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:20.020 align:middle line:84%
these laws associated with
matrix algebra computations.

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:24.140 align:middle line:84%
Everything is pretty
obvious, except maybe when

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:29.300 align:middle line:84%
you transpose a product,
you reverse the order

00:27:29.300 --> 00:27:32.780 align:middle line:90%
of the product of transposes.

00:27:32.780 --> 00:27:37.500 align:middle line:84%
And if you consider
reversing the order of matrix

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:43.080 align:middle line:84%
multiplications, if one
can reverse the order,

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:46.420 align:middle line:84%
if A and B have the same
number of rows and columns,

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:51.700 align:middle line:84%
it turns out that
the commutative law

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:55.220 align:middle line:84%
for multiplication
doesn't apply to matrices.

00:27:55.220 --> 00:28:00.860 align:middle line:84%
So you want to be careful
with those distinctions.

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:01.360 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:08.940 align:middle line:84%
So special matrices, when
we have a symmetric matrix,

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:13.280 align:middle line:84%
then its transpose
equals itself.

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:18.680 align:middle line:90%
So the matrix has basically--

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:21.240 align:middle line:90%


00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:22.760 align:middle line:90%
well, let's see.

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:27.320 align:middle line:84%
The off-diagonal entries
are matched one by one

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:30.680 align:middle line:90%
with Aij equaling Aji.

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:37.120 align:middle line:84%
The identity matrix is a matrix
with 1's along the diagonal.

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:41.480 align:middle line:84%
And it can be convenient
to represent this

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:47.360 align:middle line:84%
as the matrix of
columns where E sub

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:54.940 align:middle line:84%
j is a simple matrix
or a simple vector,

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:59.680 align:middle line:84%
which is all 0's, but
then a single 1, where

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:03.020 align:middle line:90%
this is the j-th row.

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:09.860 align:middle line:84%
And different computations
can be made easy with that

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:11.060 align:middle line:90%
sometimes.

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:17.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, there's a notation of using
the J matrix to be the matrix

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:18.550 align:middle line:90%
of all 1's.

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:22.340 align:middle line:90%


00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:29.432 align:middle line:84%
And the matrix J turns out to be
a vector 1 times its transpose.

00:29:29.432 --> 00:29:32.300 align:middle line:90%


00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:37.220 align:middle line:84%
And we have diagonal
matrices where

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:40.540 align:middle line:84%
the matrix is all 0's except
for the diagonal entries, which

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:45.780 align:middle line:84%
may or may not be 0, and are
given by the list D1 through Dn.

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:47.080 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:47.580 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:47.580 --> 00:29:55.660 align:middle line:84%
Well now, we can turn to some
applications that use matrices.

00:29:55.660 --> 00:30:02.480 align:middle line:84%
And the first one we want to
discuss is stochastic matrices.

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:11.400 align:middle line:84%
So these are matrices which will
consider a stochastic matrix

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:13.640 align:middle line:90%
that's square.

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:18.760 align:middle line:90%
And the columns of A sum to 1.

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:31.960 align:middle line:84%
So we'll have an A with
A1, A2 up to Am or An.

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:39.920 align:middle line:84%
And basically, the sum of
Aij, i equaling 1 to m,

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:41.450 align:middle line:90%
is going to equal to 1.

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:47.720 align:middle line:90%


00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:52.720 align:middle line:84%
This kind of matrix
is associated

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:56.480 align:middle line:84%
with Markov chains,
where we think of there

00:30:56.480 --> 00:30:59.320 align:middle line:90%
being m possible states.

00:30:59.320 --> 00:31:04.460 align:middle line:90%


00:31:04.460 --> 00:31:10.260 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have
m rows and m columns.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:15.900 align:middle line:84%
And we have this notation
Aij, or lowercase aij,

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:23.060 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the probability
that the state, the next state,

00:31:23.060 --> 00:31:28.300 align:middle line:84%
equals i, given
the previous state

00:31:28.300 --> 00:31:34.900 align:middle line:90%
equals j is given by this Aij.

00:31:34.900 --> 00:31:40.220 align:middle line:90%


00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:40.840 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:50.180 align:middle line:84%
For each column j, we have, if
the previous state at time t

00:31:50.180 --> 00:32:00.160 align:middle line:84%
was j, then there's a transition
to a state at time t plus 1.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:07.160 align:middle line:84%
And these transition
probabilities, in some cases,

00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:13.600 align:middle line:84%
can be assumed to be stable
or stationary over t.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:19.800 align:middle line:84%
And with a Markov chain, we
can think of there basically

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:31.880 align:middle line:84%
being different states which
are indexed from 1 up to m.

00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:43.720 align:middle line:84%
And we might think of these
as being nodes in a graph.

00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:50.280 align:middle line:84%
And there's a probability
of moving from one node

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:54.980 align:middle line:90%
to another for each time point.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:33:02.220 align:middle line:84%
And these probabilities
of transitions

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:07.300 align:middle line:84%
might be stationary in a
stationary Markov chain model.

00:33:07.300 --> 00:33:14.660 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we have a
vector, pi, which

00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:23.700 align:middle line:84%
is going to be basically pi of
t equaling pi 1 of t, pi 2 of t,

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:34.940 align:middle line:84%
down to pi m of t, so this
m vector, pi, tells us

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:43.180 align:middle line:84%
at time t what's the likelihood
of being in any of the states.

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:49.860 align:middle line:84%
Then, if we want to compute
the probabilities of states

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:53.960 align:middle line:84%
at time t plus 1,
then it turns out

00:33:53.960 --> 00:34:02.160 align:middle line:84%
to be simply the matrix vector
product of A times pi of t.

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:08.360 align:middle line:84%
So this formula
gives us the model

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:14.239 align:middle line:84%
for how the probabilities
of states occurring changes

00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:18.400 align:middle line:90%
from time t to t plus 1.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:26.520 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we consider
what is the probability

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:30.360 align:middle line:84%
vector at time t
plus 2, well, we

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:34.760 align:middle line:84%
have the same
matrix vector model

00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:38.280 align:middle line:84%
where the stationary
transition probabilities

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:44.120 align:middle line:84%
A are multiplied by the vector
of probabilities at t plus 1.

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:51.400 align:middle line:84%
And we can substitute in what
that product is, which is A pi.

00:34:51.400 --> 00:34:54.139 align:middle line:84%
And so we basically
get the square

00:34:54.139 --> 00:34:57.460 align:middle line:84%
of the transition
probability matrix

00:34:57.460 --> 00:35:02.460 align:middle line:90%
times the pi vector at time t.

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:08.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's interesting to
ask with Markov chains is,

00:35:08.380 --> 00:35:17.900 align:middle line:84%
if we start out with
a pi vector at time 0

00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:22.220 align:middle line:84%
and we have the model that the
likelihood of different states

00:35:22.220 --> 00:35:33.280 align:middle line:84%
evolves such that the t period
transition probabilities from pi

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:41.620 align:middle line:84%
0 to t is given by this formula,
then does this limit exist

00:35:41.620 --> 00:35:43.340 align:middle line:90%
or not?

00:35:43.340 --> 00:35:53.700 align:middle line:84%
And so let me just ask
that question to the class.

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:58.740 align:middle line:84%
Anyone remember from
stochastic processes perhaps

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:06.160 align:middle line:84%
or linear algebra
whether that has a limit?

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:06.840 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:08.400 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: The stationary
distribution.

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:09.900 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
If it exists, it's

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:12.720 align:middle line:90%
the stationary distribution.

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:20.600 align:middle line:84%
And so basically, if pi star
is the stationary distribution,

00:36:20.600 --> 00:36:29.520 align:middle line:84%
it is equal to a pi star so that
we're at a limiting distribution

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:33.800 align:middle line:84%
where there's no change in
probabilities, continuing

00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:40.040 align:middle line:84%
from t equal to infinity
to infinity plus 1.

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:43.120 align:middle line:90%
Then this would be the formula.

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:48.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, are there
circumstances where

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:52.700 align:middle line:84%
such a stationary
distribution doesn't exist?

00:36:52.700 --> 00:36:53.720 align:middle line:90%
Yes, OK.

00:36:53.720 --> 00:36:55.420 align:middle line:90%
And when is that?

00:36:55.420 --> 00:36:56.740 align:middle line:90%
I see some solid nods.

00:36:56.740 --> 00:36:57.606 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:36:57.606 --> 00:36:58.380 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: When it's periodic.

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:00.130 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: When
it's periodic, yep.

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:04.020 align:middle line:84%
So you could have
cycles in the process.

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:08.020 align:middle line:84%
And a simplest case
would be two states

00:37:08.020 --> 00:37:11.500 align:middle line:84%
that just alternate even odd
being one state or the other

00:37:11.500 --> 00:37:12.900 align:middle line:90%
as an example.

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:18.540 align:middle line:84%
So we need to have
acyclic distributions.

00:37:18.540 --> 00:37:22.500 align:middle line:90%


00:37:22.500 --> 00:37:28.460 align:middle line:84%
Or the Markov chain
needs to be acyclic.

00:37:28.460 --> 00:37:36.500 align:middle line:84%
And actually, all these topics
are quite interesting when

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:37.980 align:middle line:90%
you study them.

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:46.720 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that you can prove
that a Markov chain is acyclic

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:54.200 align:middle line:84%
if all of its components of the
A matrix, if every single one,

00:37:54.200 --> 00:37:58.880 align:middle line:84%
is nonzero, so
that there's always

00:37:58.880 --> 00:38:04.320 align:middle line:84%
a possibility of transitioning
from one state to another.

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:06.180 align:middle line:90%
That's a bit too restrictive.

00:38:06.180 --> 00:38:09.520 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out
that, if you consider

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:16.840 align:middle line:84%
a particular power of
A, that for some power,

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:19.480 align:middle line:84%
not necessarily very
big, but there's

00:38:19.480 --> 00:38:25.360 align:middle line:84%
at some point of looking at
powers of A, the A to that power

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:28.200 align:middle line:84%
is a matrix of all
positive entries.

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:33.840 align:middle line:84%
So it's interesting just how
these mathematical features

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:35.800 align:middle line:90%
can be shown.

00:38:35.800 --> 00:38:39.120 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:39.620 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:38:39.620 --> 00:38:44.140 align:middle line:84%
So if it does exist, we have
a stationary distribution.

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:48.360 align:middle line:90%
And that's nice.

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:50.140 align:middle line:84%
Let me just point
out that, I guess,

00:38:50.140 --> 00:38:55.100 align:middle line:84%
in the first problem set for
the course that's been posted--

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:56.760 align:middle line:90%
that's due a week from Friday.

00:38:56.760 --> 00:38:59.780 align:middle line:84%
So this is a healthy
amount of time away,

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:01.980 align:middle line:90%
I hope, for everybody--

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:06.100 align:middle line:84%
we're asking you to look at
some simple Markov chains

00:39:06.100 --> 00:39:11.440 align:middle line:84%
and explore the stationary
distribution of those cases.

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:16.260 align:middle line:84%
And the Markov
chain model is used

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:24.650 align:middle line:84%
for modeling whether, say,
trades at the buy or sell side--

00:39:24.650 --> 00:39:28.340 align:middle line:90%


00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:32.340 align:middle line:84%
weather successive
trades in a stock

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:34.240 align:middle line:84%
will be at the buy
side or sell side,

00:39:34.240 --> 00:39:37.020 align:middle line:84%
depending on what the
previous trade was at

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:37.760 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:42.078 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's move on to
another class of matrices

00:39:42.078 --> 00:39:42.870 align:middle line:90%
that's of interest.

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:48.120 align:middle line:90%


00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:50.120 align:middle line:84%
Actually, before
doing that, I'm going

00:39:50.120 --> 00:39:52.340 align:middle line:84%
to jump ahead just
in terms of topics.

00:39:52.340 --> 00:39:58.520 align:middle line:84%
But this formula for the
stationary distribution

00:39:58.520 --> 00:40:06.160 align:middle line:84%
is a formula that's very
specific to eigenvalues

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:07.212 align:middle line:90%
and eigenvectors.

00:40:07.212 --> 00:40:10.400 align:middle line:90%


00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:16.840 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think of the
matrix A having eigenvalues

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:23.440 align:middle line:84%
and eigenvectors,
what's the eigenvalue

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:25.810 align:middle line:84%
and what's the
eigenvector in this case?

00:40:25.810 --> 00:40:32.080 align:middle line:90%


00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:35.640 align:middle line:90%
Does anybody-- yes, go ahead.

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:39.620 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Eigenvalue value of 1,
and the eigenvectors would be

00:40:39.620 --> 00:40:41.820 align:middle line:90%
all the pi stars [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:44.540 align:middle line:90%


00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:46.980 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
So this basically

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:50.580 align:middle line:84%
is a case where one of
the eigenvalues is 1.

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:53.080 align:middle line:84%
Or sorry, one of the
eigenvalues is 1.

00:40:53.080 --> 00:40:56.260 align:middle line:90%
And this is satisfied.

00:40:56.260 --> 00:41:03.820 align:middle line:84%
We'll see later that,
basically, the other eigenvalues

00:41:03.820 --> 00:41:05.940 align:middle line:90%
may be smaller than 1.

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:13.380 align:middle line:84%
And when you multiply
the A matrix by itself

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:15.900 align:middle line:84%
to many, many powers,
then it's only

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:19.420 align:middle line:84%
this one eigenvector
that doesn't

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:22.640 align:middle line:90%
get shrunk to 0 in the limit.

00:41:22.640 --> 00:41:23.140 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:41:23.140 --> 00:41:27.540 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's turn to
positive matrices.

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:30.720 align:middle line:84%
Positive matrices
arise in economics,

00:41:30.720 --> 00:41:34.380 align:middle line:84%
where we think of there
being, say, prices

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:41.370 align:middle line:84%
of goods under different
states of the market

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:45.410 align:middle line:84%
or costs of goods under
states of the market.

00:41:45.410 --> 00:41:49.450 align:middle line:90%
And those are all positive.

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:59.890 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of this arising
in a single period market

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:13.450 align:middle line:84%
model, where we have n assets
indexed by i and, let's see,

00:42:13.450 --> 00:42:16.010 align:middle line:90%
we have two periods--

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:21.050 align:middle line:84%
or sorry, a single period with
a beginning and end, t0 and t

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:22.890 align:middle line:90%
equals capital T.

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:34.270 align:middle line:84%
And if we think of what is
the price of assets at time 0,

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:37.130 align:middle line:84%
we may know what
those are at time 0.

00:42:37.130 --> 00:42:41.310 align:middle line:84%
And then what are the
subsequent prices at the end

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:43.990 align:middle line:90%
of the single period?

00:42:43.990 --> 00:42:53.610 align:middle line:84%
Capital T. And those prices
may or may not be known.

00:42:53.610 --> 00:43:01.870 align:middle line:84%
And so let me just give an
example here with two assets.

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:06.460 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to think
of the two-period model.

00:43:06.460 --> 00:43:09.630 align:middle line:90%


00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:11.710 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
have n equal to 2.

00:43:11.710 --> 00:43:17.910 align:middle line:90%


00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:26.790 align:middle line:84%
We're going to consider
basically a bond, B,

00:43:26.790 --> 00:43:31.290 align:middle line:90%
which takes on Values.

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:35.210 align:middle line:90%


00:43:35.210 --> 00:43:48.370 align:middle line:84%
that will be B0 at t equals
0 and BT at time capital T.

00:43:48.370 --> 00:43:54.730 align:middle line:84%
And we'll consider the
case of a bond which

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:59.720 align:middle line:84%
basically pays interest
at the interest rate RF.

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:03.930 align:middle line:90%


00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:11.530 align:middle line:84%
So this B0 price of
the bond at time 0

00:44:11.530 --> 00:44:18.130 align:middle line:84%
grows to B sub capital T,
which is basically growing

00:44:18.130 --> 00:44:21.930 align:middle line:90%
at a simple interest rate of RF.

00:44:21.930 --> 00:44:25.130 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:35.750 align:middle line:84%
We can talk about RB, the return
of this, just being BT minus B0.

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:41.270 align:middle line:90%
So this is the absolute return.

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:49.230 align:middle line:84%
And we can also define
the percent return,

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:59.350 align:middle line:84%
which would be looking at R sub
B equal to the absolute return

00:44:59.350 --> 00:45:02.190 align:middle line:90%
divided by the initial value.

00:45:02.190 --> 00:45:06.750 align:middle line:84%
So this would be the
percentage return.

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:09.390 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:45:09.390 --> 00:45:13.270 align:middle line:84%
If we subtract this
here, we end up

00:45:13.270 --> 00:45:23.590 align:middle line:84%
getting that this
is B0 RF times T.

00:45:23.590 --> 00:45:27.270 align:middle line:84%
So this turns out to
be equal to simply RF

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:32.770 align:middle line:90%
T as the percent return.

00:45:32.770 --> 00:45:36.570 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:44.050 align:middle line:84%
We can also talk about
the simple rate of return,

00:45:44.050 --> 00:45:45.920 align:middle line:90%
which would be given by--

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:48.383 align:middle line:90%


00:45:48.383 --> 00:45:49.050 align:middle line:90%
well, let's see.

00:45:49.050 --> 00:45:54.890 align:middle line:84%
Actually, maybe, here I
should write R sub B and here

00:45:54.890 --> 00:45:58.530 align:middle line:84%
R sub B instead of
R sub F because I

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:07.930 align:middle line:84%
want to define R sub f to be
equal to basically 1 over t,

00:46:07.930 --> 00:46:10.890 align:middle line:90%
the average rate of return.

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:18.530 align:middle line:84%
So it's equal to this
times BT minus B0 over B0.

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:26.950 align:middle line:84%
And so this, simply, in this
case, is equal to that RB.

00:46:26.950 --> 00:46:34.830 align:middle line:84%
And I guess, I'll be using
a notation of characterizing

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:38.430 align:middle line:84%
an interest rate as being
a risk-free interest rate,

00:46:38.430 --> 00:46:45.310 align:middle line:84%
if this bond is guaranteed
to have that final value.

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:48.870 align:middle line:84%
And so in quantitative
finance, we often

00:46:48.870 --> 00:46:51.190 align:middle line:90%
talk about risk-free rates.

00:46:51.190 --> 00:46:55.450 align:middle line:84%
And nothing in the
world is risk-free.

00:46:55.450 --> 00:46:58.790 align:middle line:84%
But when governments
can print money

00:46:58.790 --> 00:47:02.510 align:middle line:84%
to pay off their liabilities,
then it essentially

00:47:02.510 --> 00:47:05.750 align:middle line:90%
is a risk-free rate.

00:47:05.750 --> 00:47:10.110 align:middle line:84%
And we worry less
about it with countries

00:47:10.110 --> 00:47:12.150 align:middle line:90%
with stronger economies.

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:17.510 align:middle line:90%
Now, this is the first asset.

00:47:17.510 --> 00:47:21.750 align:middle line:84%
Let's also consider
a stock, S, which

00:47:21.750 --> 00:47:26.530 align:middle line:90%
has at time 0 a value of S0.

00:47:26.530 --> 00:47:33.210 align:middle line:84%
And then at time T,
it's going to be random.

00:47:33.210 --> 00:47:40.330 align:middle line:84%
And so we have S sub
T is going to equal

00:47:40.330 --> 00:47:43.690 align:middle line:90%
different possible outcomes.

00:47:43.690 --> 00:47:45.490 align:middle line:84%
And we'll consider
a special case

00:47:45.490 --> 00:47:59.570 align:middle line:84%
where it's STu and STd, where
we have an underlying state

00:47:59.570 --> 00:48:02.970 align:middle line:90%
being an up or a down market.

00:48:02.970 --> 00:48:11.170 align:middle line:84%
So we have a random
outcome for ST.

00:48:11.170 --> 00:48:14.810 align:middle line:84%
We're not yet specifying what
probabilities are associated

00:48:14.810 --> 00:48:17.030 align:middle line:90%
with these different states.

00:48:17.030 --> 00:48:29.390 align:middle line:84%
And so there are two states
at time T, omega equal to--

00:48:29.390 --> 00:48:34.350 align:middle line:84%
well, maybe I'll just write u
and omega equal to d for that.

00:48:34.350 --> 00:48:41.430 align:middle line:84%
And so in thinking of a single
market model with n assets, not

00:48:41.430 --> 00:48:48.230 align:middle line:84%
just two, we are defining
basically two assets--

00:48:48.230 --> 00:48:54.150 align:middle line:84%
I guess I'm using j for
the assets instead of i.

00:48:54.150 --> 00:48:57.750 align:middle line:84%
But we basically have
known asset prices

00:48:57.750 --> 00:49:05.470 align:middle line:84%
at time 0 and potentially
random prices at time capital T.

00:49:05.470 --> 00:49:16.110 align:middle line:84%
And so we can model this by
associating different states

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:24.370 align:middle line:84%
or scenarios at time capital
T that are indexed w1 to wm.

00:49:24.370 --> 00:49:32.950 align:middle line:84%
And we then can have
different prices in that.

00:49:32.950 --> 00:49:36.090 align:middle line:84%
So here's the framework
for this right

00:49:36.090 --> 00:49:43.170 align:middle line:84%
now, at least the
framework with two assets.

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:50.490 align:middle line:84%
And we can also characterize
portfolios of the n

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:51.620 align:middle line:90%
equals 2 assets.

00:49:51.620 --> 00:49:54.170 align:middle line:90%


00:49:54.170 --> 00:49:58.050 align:middle line:84%
And we can define
a pi vector being

00:49:58.050 --> 00:50:02.850 align:middle line:84%
simply equal to a weight
in the bond and a weight

00:50:02.850 --> 00:50:09.090 align:middle line:84%
in the stock, units of the
stock, units of the bond,

00:50:09.090 --> 00:50:12.050 align:middle line:90%
given by this vector.

00:50:12.050 --> 00:50:21.150 align:middle line:84%
And so what we can
then do is deal

00:50:21.150 --> 00:50:27.682 align:middle line:84%
with modeling the outcomes
of this portfolio.

00:50:27.682 --> 00:50:30.910 align:middle line:90%


00:50:30.910 --> 00:50:40.430 align:middle line:84%
If we consider v0, the value
of the portfolio at time 0,

00:50:40.430 --> 00:50:48.830 align:middle line:84%
this would be just pi B
times B0 plus pi S times S0.

00:50:48.830 --> 00:50:52.110 align:middle line:90%


00:50:52.110 --> 00:51:00.670 align:middle line:84%
And at time capital
T, then our portfolio

00:51:00.670 --> 00:51:14.630 align:middle line:84%
would have values pi B BT
plus pi S times STu for the up

00:51:14.630 --> 00:51:23.130 align:middle line:84%
case and pi B BT
plus pi S times STd

00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:28.050 align:middle line:84%
when w is equal to d,
when the state is down.

00:51:28.050 --> 00:51:36.570 align:middle line:84%
So this value of the
portfolio will be random,

00:51:36.570 --> 00:51:41.220 align:middle line:84%
depending upon the likelihood
of these up and down states.

00:51:41.220 --> 00:51:43.850 align:middle line:90%


00:51:43.850 --> 00:51:52.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, with this setup,
it's useful at this point

00:51:52.530 --> 00:51:59.210 align:middle line:84%
to introduce you to
some concepts, which

00:51:59.210 --> 00:52:07.970 align:middle line:84%
is contingent claims and
replicating portfolios.

00:52:07.970 --> 00:52:17.190 align:middle line:90%


00:52:17.190 --> 00:52:26.470 align:middle line:84%
And so suppose that we
have a contingent claim

00:52:26.470 --> 00:52:38.470 align:middle line:84%
C, which has a value C0, is
the value at t equal to 0.

00:52:38.470 --> 00:52:46.030 align:middle line:84%
And Ct will be the value
of this contingent claim

00:52:46.030 --> 00:52:50.950 align:middle line:84%
depending upon whether
we have an up or down--

00:52:50.950 --> 00:52:53.420 align:middle line:84%
well, actually, I'll reverse
it, a down or an up market.

00:52:53.420 --> 00:53:03.390 align:middle line:90%


00:53:03.390 --> 00:53:08.110 align:middle line:84%
What we end up doing
in these market

00:53:08.110 --> 00:53:18.690 align:middle line:84%
models is being able to model
the prices of contingent claims

00:53:18.690 --> 00:53:23.650 align:middle line:84%
when we can replicate
the contingent claim

00:53:23.650 --> 00:53:26.340 align:middle line:84%
with a portfolio of
the underlying assets.

00:53:26.340 --> 00:53:30.690 align:middle line:90%


00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:40.530 align:middle line:84%
So we can ask, does there exist
a portfolio of B and S such

00:53:40.530 --> 00:54:12.510 align:middle line:84%
that pi Bv plus pi S STu equals
CTd and pi B plus pi S St--

00:54:12.510 --> 00:54:16.520 align:middle line:84%
I've got these notations
wrong-- equals to CTu.

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:22.630 align:middle line:90%


00:54:22.630 --> 00:54:29.440 align:middle line:90%
So if this were the case, then--

00:54:29.440 --> 00:54:32.830 align:middle line:90%


00:54:32.830 --> 00:54:44.460 align:middle line:84%
if so, then we could have C0
equaling pi B B0 plus pi S S0.

00:54:44.460 --> 00:54:47.430 align:middle line:90%


00:54:47.430 --> 00:54:52.310 align:middle line:84%
And we would replicate this
contingent claim payoff

00:54:52.310 --> 00:54:59.230 align:middle line:84%
at time capital T.
Now, mathematically,

00:54:59.230 --> 00:55:09.050 align:middle line:84%
can we solve for portfolio
coefficients that

00:55:09.050 --> 00:55:10.820 align:middle line:90%
satisfy this contingent claim?

00:55:10.820 --> 00:55:18.690 align:middle line:90%


00:55:18.690 --> 00:55:19.400 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts?

00:55:19.400 --> 00:55:26.183 align:middle line:90%


00:55:26.183 --> 00:55:26.850 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:55:26.850 --> 00:55:30.640 align:middle line:90%
If we look at CTd versus CTu--

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:34.970 align:middle line:90%


00:55:34.970 --> 00:55:36.340 align:middle line:90%
and here's the origin.

00:55:36.340 --> 00:55:39.610 align:middle line:90%


00:55:39.610 --> 00:55:47.890 align:middle line:84%
If we had a portfolio
that was just the bond,

00:55:47.890 --> 00:55:53.890 align:middle line:84%
we might have BT
here and BT here.

00:55:53.890 --> 00:55:58.970 align:middle line:84%
And here's the point
corresponding to BT, BT.

00:55:58.970 --> 00:56:01.690 align:middle line:84%
And here's the
vector corresponding

00:56:01.690 --> 00:56:04.770 align:middle line:84%
to the portfolio
that's just the bond.

00:56:04.770 --> 00:56:12.230 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at the
stock, the portfolio, that's

00:56:12.230 --> 00:56:23.350 align:middle line:84%
just the stock, we might have
STd here, and then way up here

00:56:23.350 --> 00:56:30.550 align:middle line:84%
maybe STu, the value of the
stock when the market is up.

00:56:30.550 --> 00:56:36.270 align:middle line:84%
And so this would
be our ST vector

00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:41.710 align:middle line:84%
in the two-dimensional space
corresponding to down market

00:56:41.710 --> 00:56:43.800 align:middle line:90%
state versus up market state.

00:56:43.800 --> 00:56:47.990 align:middle line:90%


00:56:47.990 --> 00:56:59.350 align:middle line:84%
Now, given this figure, if
we think of just an arbitrary

00:56:59.350 --> 00:57:04.180 align:middle line:84%
point in this space
of contingent claims,

00:57:04.180 --> 00:57:07.560 align:middle line:84%
can we construct a
portfolio that matches that?

00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:08.700 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:57:08.700 --> 00:57:11.380 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Sorry, can you
explain what [INAUDIBLE]?

00:57:11.380 --> 00:57:12.300 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes.

00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:12.800 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:12.800 --> 00:57:19.700 align:middle line:84%
The contingent claim
is a payoff that

00:57:19.700 --> 00:57:27.620 align:middle line:84%
is contingent upon the state
of the market at that time.

00:57:27.620 --> 00:57:33.620 align:middle line:84%
And it's either a down
market or an up market

00:57:33.620 --> 00:57:35.740 align:middle line:90%
over the one period.

00:57:35.740 --> 00:57:41.100 align:middle line:84%
And the payoff, if it's
an up market, is CTu.

00:57:41.100 --> 00:57:45.260 align:middle line:84%
And the payoff, if it's
a down market, is CTd.

00:57:45.260 --> 00:57:49.580 align:middle line:84%
And in this space of
contingent claims,

00:57:49.580 --> 00:57:52.100 align:middle line:84%
we can draw vectors
corresponding

00:57:52.100 --> 00:58:01.410 align:middle line:84%
to the stock and the
bond as these vectors.

00:58:01.410 --> 00:58:05.280 align:middle line:90%


00:58:05.280 --> 00:58:10.600 align:middle line:84%
And so what's curious
to think about is--

00:58:10.600 --> 00:58:16.980 align:middle line:84%
suppose we have a
contingent claim that--

00:58:16.980 --> 00:58:17.880 align:middle line:90%
well, let's see.

00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:23.150 align:middle line:84%
Suppose we consider a call
option contingent claim.

00:58:23.150 --> 00:58:30.040 align:middle line:90%


00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:30.740 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:58:30.740 --> 00:58:38.150 align:middle line:84%
So there would be
a strike price of--

00:58:38.150 --> 00:58:41.360 align:middle line:90%


00:58:41.360 --> 00:58:56.360 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- A strike price of
K to buy the stock at time T.

00:58:56.360 --> 00:59:00.060 align:middle line:84%
And in order for this
to be of interest,

00:59:00.060 --> 00:59:07.960 align:middle line:84%
we might have K being smaller
than STu and greater than STd.

00:59:07.960 --> 00:59:11.940 align:middle line:90%


00:59:11.940 --> 00:59:19.420 align:middle line:84%
So we have a call option with
strike price K to buy the stock.

00:59:19.420 --> 00:59:25.700 align:middle line:84%
And the contingent
claim of the call

00:59:25.700 --> 00:59:35.420 align:middle line:84%
would be 0, if we
have the down market.

00:59:35.420 --> 00:59:41.750 align:middle line:84%
And it would be STu minus
K if it's the up market.

00:59:41.750 --> 00:59:46.860 align:middle line:90%


00:59:46.860 --> 00:59:51.380 align:middle line:84%
So we could consider
0 in the down market.

00:59:51.380 --> 00:59:58.720 align:middle line:84%
And basically, maybe this
would be CT minus K here.

00:59:58.720 --> 01:00:05.040 align:middle line:84%
So this vector here would
correspond to the payoff

01:00:05.040 --> 01:00:06.910 align:middle line:90%
of this call option.

01:00:06.910 --> 01:00:11.960 align:middle line:90%


01:00:11.960 --> 01:00:16.000 align:middle line:84%
So under what
circumstances can we

01:00:16.000 --> 01:00:19.040 align:middle line:84%
take a linear combination
of these two vectors

01:00:19.040 --> 01:00:20.090 align:middle line:90%
and get this vector?

01:00:20.090 --> 01:00:26.840 align:middle line:90%


01:00:26.840 --> 01:00:27.880 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:00:27.880 --> 01:00:30.722 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So you've got to
short one of the stocks?

01:00:30.722 --> 01:00:31.680 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yeah.

01:00:31.680 --> 01:00:36.000 align:middle line:84%
I mean, basically,
we can solve for this

01:00:36.000 --> 01:00:44.200 align:middle line:84%
here by substituting in
for this 0 and P minus K,

01:00:44.200 --> 01:00:49.440 align:middle line:84%
and then just solve it to
get our two equations and two

01:00:49.440 --> 01:00:49.980 align:middle line:90%
unknowns.

01:00:49.980 --> 01:00:54.200 align:middle line:84%
So if this system of equations,
two equations and two unknowns,

01:00:54.200 --> 01:00:58.380 align:middle line:84%
has a solution, then
we can solve it.

01:00:58.380 --> 01:01:06.460 align:middle line:84%
And what is interesting
is to consider

01:01:06.460 --> 01:01:11.020 align:middle line:84%
what are the space of all
possible contingent claims.

01:01:11.020 --> 01:01:18.180 align:middle line:84%
And let me just
turn to this note.

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:21.020 align:middle line:90%
This is on Canvas.

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:24.020 align:middle line:84%
But this note that's been
posted in Canvas talks

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:28.360 align:middle line:84%
about a one-period economy
with two assets in section 1.

01:01:28.360 --> 01:01:31.540 align:middle line:84%
That's what I've just outlined
on the chalkboard here.

01:01:31.540 --> 01:01:35.980 align:middle line:84%
One can generalize that
to a single period model

01:01:35.980 --> 01:01:39.080 align:middle line:90%
with more than two assets.

01:01:39.080 --> 01:01:41.700 align:middle line:90%
That's chapter section 2.

01:01:41.700 --> 01:01:46.500 align:middle line:90%
And then one can generalize to--

01:01:46.500 --> 01:01:48.300 align:middle line:84%
not generalize, but
one can consider

01:01:48.300 --> 01:01:54.200 align:middle line:84%
visualizing the assets and
portfolios in section 3 here.

01:01:54.200 --> 01:02:02.080 align:middle line:84%
And so what I do in
the last section here

01:02:02.080 --> 01:02:06.240 align:middle line:84%
is looking at the
vector of payoffs

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:11.560 align:middle line:84%
of the bond and the stock,
colored differently.

01:02:11.560 --> 01:02:16.320 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a
bond that may pay off

01:02:16.320 --> 01:02:19.740 align:middle line:84%
the same amount in the
down versus the up market.

01:02:19.740 --> 01:02:25.000 align:middle line:84%
And the stock pays off more in
the up market than the down.

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:27.240 align:middle line:84%
And then one can
consider collections

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:36.480 align:middle line:84%
of portfolios that weight
the bond and the stock

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:41.320 align:middle line:84%
so that the positive
weights sum to 1.

01:02:41.320 --> 01:02:46.720 align:middle line:84%
And so these vectors correspond
to the payoff vectors

01:02:46.720 --> 01:02:51.000 align:middle line:90%
of those long portfolios.

01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:55.080 align:middle line:90%
And if we consider--

01:02:55.080 --> 01:02:59.140 align:middle line:84%
well, let's see-- looking
at those long portfolios,

01:02:59.140 --> 01:03:03.300 align:middle line:84%
instead of representing
vectors as pointed segments

01:03:03.300 --> 01:03:06.100 align:middle line:84%
from the origin, one
could just represent them

01:03:06.100 --> 01:03:08.820 align:middle line:90%
as points in the space.

01:03:08.820 --> 01:03:11.140 align:middle line:84%
And that representation
is helpful

01:03:11.140 --> 01:03:16.660 align:middle line:84%
when we want to consider a
variety of other portfolios

01:03:16.660 --> 01:03:18.020 align:middle line:90%
in the mix.

01:03:18.020 --> 01:03:28.780 align:middle line:84%
And so here's a graph showing
the value of the payoff

01:03:28.780 --> 01:03:34.300 align:middle line:84%
if we consider portfolios
that can invest up

01:03:34.300 --> 01:03:43.580 align:middle line:84%
to 100% in each of the
bond or the stock or both.

01:03:43.580 --> 01:03:48.680 align:middle line:84%
And so if we invest
100% in both,

01:03:48.680 --> 01:03:53.240 align:middle line:84%
we basically get the payoffs
that are really high.

01:03:53.240 --> 01:03:59.980 align:middle line:84%
And if we don't invest
much, close to 0 in both,

01:03:59.980 --> 01:04:02.960 align:middle line:90%
then we get the 0 vector.

01:04:02.960 --> 01:04:10.240 align:middle line:84%
But this graph shows
the contingent claims

01:04:10.240 --> 01:04:14.320 align:middle line:84%
that are mapped or
equivalent by replicating

01:04:14.320 --> 01:04:17.520 align:middle line:90%
portfolios of these types.

01:04:17.520 --> 01:04:22.360 align:middle line:84%
And so if we then
allow for shorting,

01:04:22.360 --> 01:04:25.760 align:middle line:84%
this extends the
contingent claims

01:04:25.760 --> 01:04:35.200 align:middle line:84%
to allow for negative payoffs,
which are graphed here,

01:04:35.200 --> 01:04:38.760 align:middle line:84%
basically with the payoff in
the down market versus the up.

01:04:38.760 --> 01:04:42.400 align:middle line:84%
And what's of interest
here, although it's not

01:04:42.400 --> 01:04:48.760 align:middle line:84%
quite easy to see, is
there are portfolios

01:04:48.760 --> 01:04:56.580 align:middle line:84%
where the initial value
of the portfolio is 0.

01:04:56.580 --> 01:05:03.340 align:middle line:84%
And so that would correspond
to a long short portfolio that

01:05:03.340 --> 01:05:05.290 align:middle line:90%
is implemented with zero cost.

01:05:05.290 --> 01:05:11.040 align:middle line:90%


01:05:11.040 --> 01:05:16.060 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
payoff vector at T

01:05:16.060 --> 01:05:20.140 align:middle line:84%
and looking at
the value where we

01:05:20.140 --> 01:05:26.180 align:middle line:84%
white out those portfolios
that have zero cost.

01:05:26.180 --> 01:05:29.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's useful
here to see is

01:05:29.300 --> 01:05:33.460 align:middle line:84%
that, with these
kinds of portfolios,

01:05:33.460 --> 01:05:37.220 align:middle line:84%
we can replicate
different contingent

01:05:37.220 --> 01:05:43.660 align:middle line:84%
claims which correspond to
different points in this space.

01:05:43.660 --> 01:05:55.400 align:middle line:84%
If we were able to invest more
than 100% of our portfolio,

01:05:55.400 --> 01:05:58.320 align:middle line:84%
long or short, then
we could actually

01:05:58.320 --> 01:06:05.720 align:middle line:84%
realize any point in this
space, in some cases.

01:06:05.720 --> 01:06:10.160 align:middle line:84%
And so any contingent
claim could be solved

01:06:10.160 --> 01:06:16.000 align:middle line:90%
for with this equation here.

01:06:16.000 --> 01:06:18.880 align:middle line:90%
And that's rather neat.

01:06:18.880 --> 01:06:23.000 align:middle line:84%
But it also is
reasonable to consider

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:26.120 align:middle line:84%
if we make certain assumptions
on the market, such

01:06:26.120 --> 01:06:31.800 align:middle line:84%
as, as you buy more
of an asset, you don't

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:35.360 align:middle line:90%
change the price of the asset.

01:06:35.360 --> 01:06:37.680 align:middle line:84%
There's basically a
fixed price is suitable

01:06:37.680 --> 01:06:38.980 align:middle line:90%
regardless of how much.

01:06:38.980 --> 01:06:44.500 align:middle line:84%
And there's no constraint
on short selling the asset,

01:06:44.500 --> 01:06:47.120 align:middle line:84%
that you can do
that with liberty.

01:06:47.120 --> 01:06:52.060 align:middle line:84%
So there are these kinds
of properties that arise.

01:06:52.060 --> 01:06:57.500 align:middle line:84%
Now, with the case of
extending from the bond

01:06:57.500 --> 01:07:01.820 align:middle line:90%
and the single stock to m--

01:07:01.820 --> 01:07:07.580 align:middle line:84%
sorry, little n assets,
then everything generalizes.

01:07:07.580 --> 01:07:15.220 align:middle line:84%
And we end up having a
positive price matrix at time T

01:07:15.220 --> 01:07:20.100 align:middle line:84%
corresponding to different
payoffs of the individual assets

01:07:20.100 --> 01:07:28.260 align:middle line:84%
at time capital T for different
states omega 1 through omega m.

01:07:28.260 --> 01:07:41.840 align:middle line:84%
And with this kind of setup, one
can represent the initial value

01:07:41.840 --> 01:07:51.240 align:middle line:84%
of a portfolio Q being
the sum of the Q weights

01:07:51.240 --> 01:07:55.000 align:middle line:84%
times the initial
prices in the A0 vector.

01:07:55.000 --> 01:08:01.080 align:middle line:84%
And then the payoff of the
portfolio at time capital T

01:08:01.080 --> 01:08:07.720 align:middle line:84%
is given by the same weights qj
times the time capital T prices.

01:08:07.720 --> 01:08:10.000 align:middle line:84%
But we have different
prices depending

01:08:10.000 --> 01:08:15.160 align:middle line:90%
upon what state occurs.

01:08:15.160 --> 01:08:21.880 align:middle line:84%
And so in this case, we
have basically our matrix

01:08:21.880 --> 01:08:29.040 align:middle line:84%
A times Q giving us the vector
of state-dependent values

01:08:29.040 --> 01:08:37.220 align:middle line:84%
at time capital T. And
so with this model,

01:08:37.220 --> 01:08:43.939 align:middle line:84%
we can contemplate there
being an arbitrage portfolio

01:08:43.939 --> 01:08:50.100 align:middle line:84%
where the initial
value is not positive.

01:08:50.100 --> 01:08:52.640 align:middle line:84%
I guess one could
constrain this to be 0.

01:08:52.640 --> 01:08:55.129 align:middle line:84%
But it's easier to just say
less than or equal to 0.

01:08:55.129 --> 01:08:59.020 align:middle line:90%


01:08:59.020 --> 01:09:00.779 align:middle line:84%
If we have a negative
cost, that means

01:09:00.779 --> 01:09:06.060 align:middle line:84%
we get paid to implement
this portfolio.

01:09:06.060 --> 01:09:09.779 align:middle line:84%
And it will be an
arbitrage portfolio

01:09:09.779 --> 01:09:19.460 align:middle line:84%
if the likelihood of
a negative payoff is 0

01:09:19.460 --> 01:09:22.989 align:middle line:84%
and there's some likelihood
of a positive payoff.

01:09:22.989 --> 01:09:25.899 align:middle line:90%


01:09:25.899 --> 01:09:29.899 align:middle line:84%
Such a portfolio
would be risk-free,

01:09:29.899 --> 01:09:32.180 align:middle line:90%
in terms of downside risk.

01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:33.899 align:middle line:90%
We never lose money.

01:09:33.899 --> 01:09:38.340 align:middle line:84%
We either make money all
the time in at least one

01:09:38.340 --> 01:09:39.960 align:middle line:90%
of the states.

01:09:39.960 --> 01:09:44.000 align:middle line:84%
So this is an
arbitrage portfolio.

01:09:44.000 --> 01:09:52.200 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this
model, there can be

01:09:52.200 --> 01:09:55.760 align:middle line:84%
conditions that
ensure that there

01:09:55.760 --> 01:09:59.080 align:middle line:90%
is no arbitrage in the market.

01:09:59.080 --> 01:10:08.080 align:middle line:84%
And so that's one
issue to think about.

01:10:08.080 --> 01:10:12.360 align:middle line:84%
What conditions would
ensure no arbitrage?

01:10:12.360 --> 01:10:16.240 align:middle line:84%
And another condition
that's of interest

01:10:16.240 --> 01:10:21.280 align:middle line:84%
is called market
completeness, which is,

01:10:21.280 --> 01:10:27.560 align:middle line:84%
are there portfolios that can
realize any payoff vector?

01:10:27.560 --> 01:10:33.560 align:middle line:84%
So in looking at the
contingent claims payoff,

01:10:33.560 --> 01:10:40.420 align:middle line:84%
can we span this space
fully with portfolios

01:10:40.420 --> 01:10:44.060 align:middle line:84%
of the underlying
assets in the market?

01:10:44.060 --> 01:10:50.460 align:middle line:84%
And so if we can represent
any contingent payoff

01:10:50.460 --> 01:10:56.260 align:middle line:84%
as a portfolio, then we say
that the market is complete.

01:10:56.260 --> 01:11:07.180 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that the
conditions of no arbitrage

01:11:07.180 --> 01:11:12.140 align:middle line:84%
and market completeness are
related to whether a pricing

01:11:12.140 --> 01:11:16.980 align:middle line:90%
measure exists on scenarios.

01:11:16.980 --> 01:11:23.780 align:middle line:84%
And so think about Q star being
a probability distribution

01:11:23.780 --> 01:11:29.300 align:middle line:84%
over the underlying states,
omega 1 through omega m.

01:11:29.300 --> 01:11:44.960 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that if the
initial price of the j-th asset

01:11:44.960 --> 01:11:49.040 align:middle line:84%
is the discounted
expected terminal

01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:55.440 align:middle line:84%
price of that j-th asset
across the different states,

01:11:55.440 --> 01:12:00.120 align:middle line:84%
then Q star is called
a pricing measure.

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:05.240 align:middle line:84%
So our initial prices
equal a discount

01:12:05.240 --> 01:12:12.790 align:middle line:84%
factor times the expected
terminal time t values.

01:12:12.790 --> 01:12:15.600 align:middle line:90%


01:12:15.600 --> 01:12:19.080 align:middle line:84%
If such a pricing
measure exists,

01:12:19.080 --> 01:12:21.986 align:middle line:90%
then there's no arbitrage.

01:12:21.986 --> 01:12:26.820 align:middle line:90%


01:12:26.820 --> 01:12:32.200 align:middle line:84%
So it's no arbitrage if all
of the qj stars are positive.

01:12:32.200 --> 01:12:35.300 align:middle line:84%
So if this pricing measure
gives positive weight

01:12:35.300 --> 01:12:37.660 align:middle line:84%
to each of the
underlying states,

01:12:37.660 --> 01:12:39.980 align:middle line:90%
then there's no arbitrage.

01:12:39.980 --> 01:12:45.300 align:middle line:84%
And we say that the market
is complete with no arbitrage

01:12:45.300 --> 01:12:48.620 align:middle line:84%
if this pricing
measure is unique.

01:12:48.620 --> 01:12:55.180 align:middle line:84%
So it's an interesting
set of properties

01:12:55.180 --> 01:13:01.660 align:middle line:84%
that actually are quite,
well, useful to know about.

01:13:01.660 --> 01:13:05.020 align:middle line:84%
And these models
actually underlie

01:13:05.020 --> 01:13:12.020 align:middle line:84%
much of option pricing theory
and arbitrage relationships

01:13:12.020 --> 01:13:13.510 align:middle line:90%
in quantitative finance.

01:13:13.510 --> 01:13:16.340 align:middle line:90%


01:13:16.340 --> 01:13:22.420 align:middle line:84%
And in this single
period model, note

01:13:22.420 --> 01:13:27.780 align:middle line:84%
that we have distributed there's
this reference of this book

01:13:27.780 --> 01:13:33.050 align:middle line:84%
by Albanese and
Campolieti, where they

01:13:33.050 --> 01:13:34.570 align:middle line:90%
go through proofs of these.

01:13:34.570 --> 01:13:36.230 align:middle line:84%
And they can get
a bit technical.

01:13:36.230 --> 01:13:42.490 align:middle line:84%
But the basic ideas, I think,
are relatively accessible.

01:13:42.490 --> 01:13:46.130 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

01:13:46.130 --> 01:13:53.690 align:middle line:84%
Moving on to other
applications of matrices,

01:13:53.690 --> 01:13:59.490 align:middle line:84%
we can consider systems
of linear equations

01:13:59.490 --> 01:14:03.370 align:middle line:84%
and their solution,
where we basically

01:14:03.370 --> 01:14:12.810 align:middle line:90%
have m equations in n unknowns.

01:14:12.810 --> 01:14:25.610 align:middle line:84%
And so with these m equations,
we have Ax equals B, a B vector.

01:14:25.610 --> 01:14:30.630 align:middle line:84%
And to solve such a
system of equations,

01:14:30.630 --> 01:14:34.630 align:middle line:84%
well, one case where
we can solve these

01:14:34.630 --> 01:14:39.590 align:middle line:84%
is where our number of unknowns
and number of equations

01:14:39.590 --> 01:14:43.930 align:middle line:84%
are the same and our
coefficients matrix

01:14:43.930 --> 01:14:48.430 align:middle line:84%
A in the system of
equations has full rank.

01:14:48.430 --> 01:14:54.350 align:middle line:84%
So A being square
and of full rank

01:14:54.350 --> 01:14:57.670 align:middle line:84%
has a determinant
that's non-zero

01:14:57.670 --> 01:15:02.670 align:middle line:90%
and its inverse matrix exists.

01:15:02.670 --> 01:15:06.910 align:middle line:84%
And so we can actually just
take our system of equations

01:15:06.910 --> 01:15:15.710 align:middle line:84%
and multiply both sides by the
inverse of A and get our answer.

01:15:15.710 --> 01:15:20.110 align:middle line:84%
When the number of
equations is smaller

01:15:20.110 --> 01:15:23.710 align:middle line:84%
than the number of
unknowns, then we

01:15:23.710 --> 01:15:27.290 align:middle line:84%
have an undetermined
system of equations.

01:15:27.290 --> 01:15:30.610 align:middle line:84%
When m is greater than n, it's
overdetermined and so forth.

01:15:30.610 --> 01:15:33.130 align:middle line:84%
So these are different
cases that you likely

01:15:33.130 --> 01:15:36.800 align:middle line:84%
are familiar with from your
study of linear algebra.

01:15:36.800 --> 01:15:39.770 align:middle line:90%


01:15:39.770 --> 01:15:41.610 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:15:41.610 --> 01:15:46.050 align:middle line:84%
The next topic in our
remaining few minutes

01:15:46.050 --> 01:15:51.930 align:middle line:90%
is eigenvalues and eigenvectors.

01:15:51.930 --> 01:15:57.010 align:middle line:84%
When we have square
matrices A, we

01:15:57.010 --> 01:16:03.170 align:middle line:84%
can consider the existence
of an eigenvalue lambda

01:16:03.170 --> 01:16:10.490 align:middle line:84%
and an eigenvector V such
that the matrix A times

01:16:10.490 --> 01:16:16.290 align:middle line:84%
v is equal to just the
scalar lambda times v. So

01:16:16.290 --> 01:16:24.590 align:middle line:84%
the matrix vector product just
rescales v by the factor lambda.

01:16:24.590 --> 01:16:30.430 align:middle line:84%
And one can solve for
eigenvalues and eigenvectors

01:16:30.430 --> 01:16:34.950 align:middle line:84%
by just re-expressing the
eigenvalue-eigenvector

01:16:34.950 --> 01:16:40.110 align:middle line:84%
relationship as this
system of equations.

01:16:40.110 --> 01:16:47.340 align:middle line:84%
And so this system of
equations will correspond to--

01:16:47.340 --> 01:16:51.510 align:middle line:90%


01:16:51.510 --> 01:16:53.110 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:16:53.110 --> 01:17:00.230 align:middle line:84%
This system of
equations can be solved

01:17:00.230 --> 01:17:05.670 align:middle line:84%
by saying that the determinant
of A minus lambda i

01:17:05.670 --> 01:17:07.790 align:middle line:90%
is equal to 0.

01:17:07.790 --> 01:17:16.630 align:middle line:84%
And so if lambda equals 1 of
the roots of this polynomial,

01:17:16.630 --> 01:17:25.130 align:middle line:84%
then that lambda will
be an eigenvalue.

01:17:25.130 --> 01:17:29.850 align:middle line:84%
And one can then, for
that fixed eigenvalue,

01:17:29.850 --> 01:17:34.720 align:middle line:84%
solve for the eigenvector
v that satisfies that.

01:17:34.720 --> 01:17:37.850 align:middle line:90%


01:17:37.850 --> 01:17:43.050 align:middle line:84%
Now, there are properties with
eigenvalues and eigenvectors

01:17:43.050 --> 01:17:47.330 align:middle line:84%
that are very
useful, one of which

01:17:47.330 --> 01:17:56.650 align:middle line:84%
is that the maximum eigenvalue
magnitude corresponds

01:17:56.650 --> 01:18:04.200 align:middle line:84%
to the maximum length of Av for
vectors v that are of length 1.

01:18:04.200 --> 01:18:06.770 align:middle line:90%


01:18:06.770 --> 01:18:16.890 align:middle line:84%
And when we have a matrix A
that has independent eigenvalues

01:18:16.890 --> 01:18:22.450 align:middle line:84%
or eigenvectors, then,
in this special case,

01:18:22.450 --> 01:18:24.510 align:middle line:84%
we can consider
defining the matrix

01:18:24.510 --> 01:18:32.310 align:middle line:84%
S to be the matrix whose columns
are the different eigenvectors.

01:18:32.310 --> 01:18:36.850 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
this equation here,

01:18:36.850 --> 01:18:43.190 align:middle line:84%
if we multiply the original
matrix A by this S matrix,

01:18:43.190 --> 01:18:49.390 align:middle line:84%
then each column of the product
is the corresponding eigenvalue

01:18:49.390 --> 01:18:52.030 align:middle line:84%
times the corresponding
eigenvector.

01:18:52.030 --> 01:18:57.990 align:middle line:84%
This equation here is
very straightforward

01:18:57.990 --> 01:19:01.710 align:middle line:90%
from the notation above.

01:19:01.710 --> 01:19:08.270 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider the
inverse of this S matrix

01:19:08.270 --> 01:19:14.310 align:middle line:84%
that exists if the eigenvectors
are linearly independent,

01:19:14.310 --> 01:19:20.370 align:middle line:84%
then we can write A to
be S lambda S inverse.

01:19:20.370 --> 01:19:27.510 align:middle line:84%
And this representation
of the matrix A

01:19:27.510 --> 01:19:32.210 align:middle line:84%
also gives us the way of
diagonalizing A by pre

01:19:32.210 --> 01:19:37.530 align:middle line:84%
and post-multiplying by S
inverse and S respectively.

01:19:37.530 --> 01:19:42.570 align:middle line:84%
And so with powers of a
diagonalizable matrix A,

01:19:42.570 --> 01:19:45.450 align:middle line:90%
they have a very nice form.

01:19:45.450 --> 01:19:51.050 align:middle line:84%
We basically get cancellation of
S And S inverse in the products

01:19:51.050 --> 01:19:52.970 align:middle line:90%
of A times itself.

01:19:52.970 --> 01:20:02.210 align:middle line:84%
And so powers of A simply are
the same matrix S times lambda,

01:20:02.210 --> 01:20:05.410 align:middle line:84%
the diagonal matrix of
eigenvalues to the k-th power

01:20:05.410 --> 01:20:07.130 align:middle line:90%
times S inverse.

01:20:07.130 --> 01:20:15.170 align:middle line:84%
And so these are very useful
in defining state equations

01:20:15.170 --> 01:20:16.300 align:middle line:90%
and Kalman filters.

01:20:16.300 --> 01:20:19.390 align:middle line:90%


01:20:19.390 --> 01:20:20.390 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:20:20.390 --> 01:20:22.670 align:middle line:90%
So let me just--

01:20:22.670 --> 01:20:24.990 align:middle line:90%
I guess I'll finish there.

01:20:24.990 --> 01:20:28.030 align:middle line:84%
There's some
interesting key theorems

01:20:28.030 --> 01:20:29.490 align:middle line:90%
in the remainder of these notes.

01:20:29.490 --> 01:20:32.830 align:middle line:90%
I think they're pretty readable.

01:20:32.830 --> 01:20:37.270 align:middle line:84%
And I'll maybe point out
these during our next lecture.

01:20:37.270 --> 01:20:40.030 align:middle line:90%
But there we are.

01:20:40.030 --> 01:20:49.030 align:middle line:84%
Also, let's see, I
posted an RStudio program

01:20:49.030 --> 01:20:57.070 align:middle line:84%
that computes portfolios
of S&P 500 stocks and looks

01:20:57.070 --> 01:21:00.030 align:middle line:90%
at the changing value over time.

01:21:00.030 --> 01:21:05.830 align:middle line:84%
And so I hope people can
explore those programs

01:21:05.830 --> 01:21:09.940 align:middle line:84%
and try to use those
in the RStudio Cloud.

01:21:09.940 --> 01:21:16.000 align:middle line:90%